ld run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.  Hence **\\(k\\ge4\\) is also impossible**.\n\nCombining the two observations we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k\\neq2,\\;k\\neq4,5,\\dots,\n\\]\n\nso the only candidates are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines realising the three remaining values of \\(k\\).\n\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\ntogether with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,(x-n) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n*Coverage check*:\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-1\\), then it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* The unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); substituting \\(x=n\\) into (4) gives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n#### 4.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nDefine three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)      &&\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-1=-2\\,(x-(n-1))        &&\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-1=   \\,(x-(n-2))      &&\\text{through }(n-2,1)\\text{ and }(n-1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\) are all sunny.  \nThe six points listed in (6) are verified directly:\n\n* \\(L_1\\): for \\((n,1)\\) we have \\(1-1=0\\); for \\((n-2,2)\\) we have \\(2-1=-\\tfrac12\\,(n-2-n)=1\\), both satisfy the equation.\n* \\(L_2\\): for \\((n-1,1)\\) we have \\(1