(5,1)\n\nWe need to cover all these points with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe can try to use a horizontal line y=1 as a sunny line? No, horizontal is non-sunny. So we cannot use horizontal. So we cannot cover the bottom row (y=1) with a non-sunny line (since we only have one non-sunny line, which we used as vertical x=1). So we must cover points with y=1 in columns 2-5 using sunny lines. That means each of those points must be covered by a sunny line. Since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, we need to assign each of those points to distinct sunny lines (or possibly the same line if it passes through multiple of them, but that would be a horizontal line, which is not allowed). So each point (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1) must be covered by distinct sunny lines. Since we have exactly 4 sunny lines, each of those lines must cover one of those points. So each sunny line will have at least one point: (2,1) on line A, (3,1) on line B, (4,1) on line C, (5,1) on line D.\n\nNow we need to also cover the other points in each column using the same lines (since we have no more lines). Each line can intersect each column at most once, so line A (covering (2,1)) can also intersect column 3 at some point (3,y) (different from (3,1) because line A already intersects column 3 at (3,1) if it passes through (3,1) as well, but that would be a line through (2,1) and (3,1) which is horizontal, not allowed). So line A cannot also intersect column 3 at (3,1). It could intersect column 3 at a different point (3,y) with y ≠ 1. Similarly for other columns.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines, each passing through a distinct point in the bottom row (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1), and also passing through other points in other columns, covering all points.\n\nLet's denote lines:\n\nLine A: passes through (2,1) and some point in column 3 (3,y3_A), column 4 (4,y4_A), column 5 (5,y5_A). It cannot be horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. So slope must not be 0, ∞, -1.\n\nLine B: passes through (3,1) and points in columns 2,4,5 maybe.\n\nLine C: passes through (4,1) and points in columns 2,3,5.\n\nLine D: passes through (5,1) and points in columns 2,3,4.\n\nWe need to assign points such that each point in each column is covered exactly once.\n\nLet's list all points to cover:\n\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\nColumn 4: (4,1),(4,2)\nColumn 5: (5,1)\n\nWe already assign (2,1) to line A, (3,1) to line B, (4,1) to line C, (5,1) to line D.\n\nNow we need to cover the remaining points:\n\nColumn 2: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4) need to be covered by lines B, C, D (since line A already used column 2 at (2,1)). Each of those lines can intersect column 2 at at most one point. So we need to assign (2,2) to line B, (2,3) to line C, (2,4) to line D (or any permutation). Similarly, column 3: remaining points (3,2),(3,3) need to be covered by lines A, C, D (since line B already used column 3 at (3,1)). So assign (3,2) to line A, (3,3) to line C (or D). Column 4: remaining point (4,2) needs to be covered by lines A, B, D (since line C used column 4 at (4,1)). So assign (4,2) to line A (or B or D). Column 5: no remaining points (since only (5,1) existed). So we have assignments:\n\n- Line A: (2,1), (3,2), (4,2) maybe also (5,?) none.\n- Line B: (3,1), (2,2)\n- Line C: (4,1), (2,3), (3,3)\n- Line D: (5,1), (2,4)\n\nNow we need to check if each line can be made sunny (i.e., slope not 0, ∞, -1) while passing through its assigned points.\n\nLine A: points (2,1), (3,2), (4,2). Let's see if these three points are collinear. Compute slopes: slope between (2,1) and (3,2) = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1. Slope between (3,2) and (4,2) = (2-2)/(4-3) = 0. So they are not collinear. So we cannot have a line passing through all three points. But we could assign only two points per line, and the third point could be covered by another line. However, we need to cover all points, and each line can intersect each column at most once, but we can have lines that intersect only a subset of columns